IMPORT prelude
IMPORT `hsa-charges`

-- Health Services Act 2016 (WA), with the Health Services (Fees and Charges) Order 2016 -- the Q&A layer: the questions
-- people ask about what a WA public hospital may charge, with the seat and the situation as parameters, for the site's
-- question form.
--
-- PURPOSE. One deployment serves every seat. Every exported question takes `my role` first ("Which of these describes
-- you"). A question about another role answers `does not arise for you` without reading any other input, so the form never
-- asks those inputs and drops the question. The baseline's questions take a record (`Me as a patient`) and, in one case,
-- the baseline's `Charge`; here every input is one plain fact, and the records are built inside L4.
--
-- ROLES. (1) `I am a patient of a WA public health service`: the baseline's seat, the person charged (s 57(1)).
-- (2) `I am a family member or carer of a patient, or stay in hospital with one`: the person the Order charges for staying
-- with a patient (Schedule 1 Division 1 item 7), and to whom the chief executive may disclose the patient's health
-- information (s 217(2)). (3) `I must pay compensation to someone a public hospital treated for their injury`: the
-- "compensation payer" (s 53A), a person, not only an insurer, from whom the hospital may recover its compensable charge
-- (s 57A(3)(a), (4), (5)) and who must pay it first once notified (s 57D). (4) `I hold information about someone that I
-- obtained through the WA health system`: anyone bound by the confidentiality offence (s 219(1)), such as a person who
-- works for a health service provider, or a relative told under s 217. The Minister, the Department CEO, health service
-- providers and their chief executives are never seats; what they may or must do appears as what it means for the person.
--
-- SITUATIONS (as in the Graffiti Vandalism Act layer, design D8). Within a role, a question that arises only in a situation
-- takes that situation's fact straight after `my role` and answers `does not arise for you` without reading anything else
-- when it does not hold; the questions of one situation share one input. The patient's: how I attend (the per-day and
-- whole-stay charges do not arise for an out-patient; the out-patient service and medicine charges arise only for one);
-- a payment for my injury (ss 53B, 57A, 57D). The family member's: staying in the hospital with the patient (item 7). The
-- compensation payer's: another person also liable (s 57A(4)).
--
-- The rules are in hsa-charges.l4. For the patient this module calls them and restates none; each answer equals the
-- baseline export's wherever the role and situation hold. The other roles use the baseline's rules where they exist (item 7,
-- ss 57D, 217, 219, 220) and helpers below, quoted from the deposit, where they do not (s 57A(3)(a), (4), (5)).
-- No exported question takes a DEONTIC or EVENT input.
--
-- AMOUNTS as in the baseline: the Order's figures in force from 1 July 2026.
--
-- BASELINE. Not for public use as advice. See NOTES.md, "Q&A layer (seat and situations as parameters)".

§ `Health Services Act 2016 (WA) -- hospital charges: the questions people ask`

§§ `Who is asking, and the shape of the answers`

DECLARE Role IS ONE OF
    `I am a patient of a WA public health service`
    `I am a family member or carer of a patient, or stay in hospital with one`
    `I must pay compensation to someone a public hospital treated for their injury`
    `I hold information about someone that I obtained through the WA health system`

DECLARE `Yes or no` IS ONE OF
    yes
    no
    `does not arise for you`

DECLARE `Amount` IS ONE OF
    `does not arise for you`
    amount HAS dollars IS A NUMBER

-- The baseline's own answer types, each carried inside an answer that can also say `does not arise for you`.
DECLARE `Charge answer` IS ONE OF
    `does not arise for you`
    `the hospital's charge` HAS `the charge` IS A `Charge`

DECLARE `Class answer` IS ONE OF
    `does not arise for you`
    `my class is` HAS `the class` IS A `My class`

GIVEN `it holds` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A `Yes or no`
`yes if` `it holds` MEANS
    IF `it holds` THEN yes ELSE no

§§ `The situations a question arises in`

-- The patient's attendance (Order cl 11(1)) is the baseline's `How I attend`. It is the situation of the per-day and
-- whole-stay charges (not for an out-patient, who is charged per service) and of the out-patient service and medicine
-- charges (only for an out-patient); elsewhere it is an ordinary input. This helper lists every value and has no
-- OTHERWISE: on the stable l4 a CONSIDER with OTHERWISE quietly takes the OTHERWISE branch for an ASSUMEd value (Graffiti
-- Vandalism Act layer, finding 31), so the cases could not show that an ASSUMEd situation is never read.
GIVEN `how I attend` IS A `How I attend`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `I attend as an out-patient` IF
    CONSIDER `how I attend`
    WHEN `as an in-patient` THEN FALSE
    WHEN `as a day patient` THEN FALSE
    WHEN `as an out-patient` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `as a same day patient` THEN FALSE

§§ `Building the baseline's record from plain answers`

GIVEN `eligible` IS A BOOLEAN
      `compensation right` IS A BOOLEAN
      `decided not compensable` IS A BOOLEAN
      `motor vehicle insurance` IS A BOOLEAN
      `other compensation law` IS A BOOLEAN
      `DVA veteran` IS A BOOLEAN
      `nursing home type` IS A BOOLEAN
      `election` IS A `My election`
      `asylum seeker` IS A BOOLEAN
      `involuntary` IS A BOOLEAN
      `Mental Health Act treatment` IS A BOOLEAN
      `s 6(2) declaration` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A `Me as a patient`
`me from my answers` MEANS `Me as a patient` WITH
    `I am an eligible person under the Health Insurance Act 1973` IS `eligible`
    `I have, or appear to have, a right to compensation for this condition` IS `compensation right`
    `the chief executive has decided I am not a compensable patient` IS `decided not compensable`
    `motor vehicle third party insurance applies or appears to apply` IS `motor vehicle insurance`
    `workers compensation, catastrophic injuries or Defence Force law applies or appears to apply` IS `other compensation law`
    `I am a veteran who elects the DVA arrangement` IS `DVA veteran`
    `I am a nursing home type patient` IS `nursing home type`
    `my election` IS `election`
    `I am an asylum seeker` IS `asylum seeker`
    `I am an involuntary patient under the Mental Health Act 2014` IS `involuntary`
    `the service is part of my Mental Health Act treatment` IS `Mental Health Act treatment`
    `a declaration under s 6(2) of the Health Insurance Act 1973 applies to me` IS `s 6(2) declaration`

§§ `For the patient`

§§§ `ss 7, 8, 55(1) -- is this the public system, and may I be charged at all`

@export Is my care a public health service (Health Services Act 2016 (WA) s 7(3), (4))?
GIVEN `my role` IS A Role @desc Which of these describes you
      `provider` IS A `Who provides my health service` @desc Who gives you the care: a WA health service provider (an area health service), the Department CEO, a contracted health entity under a contract with one of them or the State, or a private provider with no such contract
      `declared public by regulation` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a regulation declares this service to be a public health service
      `declared not public by regulation` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a regulation declares this service not to be a public health service
GIVETH A `Yes or no`
`Is the care I receive a public health service` MEANS
    CONSIDER `my role`
    WHEN `I am a patient of a WA public health service` THEN `yes if` (`Is my care a public health service` `provider` `declared public by regulation` `declared not public by regulation`)
    WHEN `I am a family member or carer of a patient, or stay in hospital with one` THEN `does not arise for you`
    WHEN `I must pay compensation to someone a public hospital treated for their injury` THEN `does not arise for you`
    WHEN `I hold information about someone that I obtained through the WA health system` THEN `does not arise for you`

@export Is the hospital a public hospital (Health Services Act 2016 (WA) s 8(6)-(8))?
GIVEN `my role` IS A Role @desc Which of these describes you
      `controlled or managed by a health service provider or the Department CEO` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a WA health service provider or the Department runs the hospital
      `declared a public hospital by the Minister` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a Ministerial order in the Gazette declares it a public hospital (s 8(7))
      `declared not a public hospital by the Minister` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a Ministerial order in the Gazette declares it not a public hospital (s 8(8))
GIVETH A `Yes or no`
`Is my hospital a public hospital` MEANS
    CONSIDER `my role`
    WHEN `I am a patient of a WA public health service` THEN `yes if` (`Is the hospital a public hospital` `controlled or managed by a health service provider or the Department CEO` `declared a public hospital by the Minister` `declared not a public hospital by the Minister`)
    WHEN `I am a family member or carer of a patient, or stay in hospital with one` THEN `does not arise for you`
    WHEN `I must pay compensation to someone a public hospital treated for their injury` THEN `does not arise for you`
    WHEN `I hold information about someone that I obtained through the WA health system` THEN `does not arise for you`

@export May the health service provider charge me for my health service at all (Health Services Act 2016 (WA) s 55(1))?
GIVEN `my role` IS A Role @desc Which of these describes you
      `provider` IS A `Who provides my health service` @desc Who gives you the care: a WA health service provider (an area health service), the Department CEO, a contracted health entity under a contract with one of them or the State, or a private provider with no such contract
      `the National Health Agreement says I am not to be charged` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether it has been agreed under the National Health Agreement that you are not to be charged for this service
      `the Agreement is in force` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the National Health Agreement is in force when the service is given
      `a Ministerial order says no fees are payable` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether an order under s 56(2)(b) in force when the service is given says no fees or charges are payable for it (for example Order cl 6A)
GIVETH A `Yes or no`
`May the health service provider charge me at all` MEANS
    CONSIDER `my role`
    WHEN `I am a patient of a WA public health service` THEN `yes if` (`May the hospital charge me at all` `provider` `the National Health Agreement says I am not to be charged` `the Agreement is in force` `a Ministerial order says no fees are payable`)
    WHEN `I am a family member or carer of a patient, or stay in hospital with one` THEN `does not arise for you`
    WHEN `I must pay compensation to someone a public hospital treated for their injury` THEN `does not arise for you`
    WHEN `I hold information about someone that I obtained through the WA health system` THEN `does not arise for you`

§§§ `Order cll 3, 3A, 6A, 11-15 -- what class of patient am I`

@export Am I a compensable patient (Health Services (Fees and Charges) Order 2016 (WA) cll 3A, 12(3), 13(3), 14(5), 15(5))?
GIVEN `my role` IS A Role @desc Which of these describes you
      `how I attend` IS A `How I attend` @desc Whether you are an in-patient (admitted to a hospital bed), a day patient (at a day hospital), an out-patient, or a same day patient (admitted and discharged on the same day)
      `I am an eligible person under the Health Insurance Act 1973` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are an "eligible person" for Medicare under the Health Insurance Act 1973 s 3 (the Health Insurance Act encoding works this out)
      `I have, or appear to have, a right to compensation for this condition` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have received, or established a right to receive, compensation (such as workers' compensation, motor vehicle third party insurance or damages) for the injury, illness or disease you are treated for, or appear to have that right when you attend
      `the chief executive has decided I am not a compensable patient` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the hospital's chief executive has decided under Order cl 3A(5) that you are not a compensable patient
      `motor vehicle third party insurance applies or appears to apply` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Motor Vehicle (Third Party Insurance) Act 1943 applies, or appears to apply, to the payment for your treatment
      `workers compensation, catastrophic injuries or Defence Force law applies or appears to apply` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Workers Compensation and Injury Management Act 2023, the Motor Vehicle and Workplace Accidents (Catastrophic Injuries) Act 2016 or the law relating to the Defence Force applies, or appears to apply, to the payment for your treatment
      `I am a veteran who elects the DVA arrangement` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are a veteran who elects to be treated under the arrangement with the Department of Veterans' Affairs
      `I am a nursing home type patient` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are a nursing home type patient (a Health Insurance Act 1973 term for a long-stay in-patient who no longer needs acute care)
      `my election` IS A `My election` @desc Whether, on admission, you elected to be treated as a public or a private patient (an eligible in-patient or same day patient must elect)
      `I am an asylum seeker` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have applied for a permanent or temporary protection visa or a safe haven enterprise visa, and the application has not yet been decided
      `I am an involuntary patient under the Mental Health Act 2014` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are under an inpatient or community treatment order, another detaining order, or an unexpired referral under the Mental Health Act 2014
      `the service is part of my Mental Health Act treatment` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether this service is part of your treatment under the Mental Health Act 2014
      `a declaration under s 6(2) of the Health Insurance Act 1973 applies to me` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a declaration under the Health Insurance Act 1973 s 6(2) applies to you, so that you are treated as not an eligible person
GIVETH A `Yes or no`
`Am I a compensable patient for hospital charges` MEANS
    CONSIDER `my role`
    WHEN `I am a patient of a WA public health service` THEN `yes if` (`Am I a compensable patient` (`me from my answers` `I am an eligible person under the Health Insurance Act 1973` `I have, or appear to have, a right to compensation for this condition` `the chief executive has decided I am not a compensable patient` `motor vehicle third party insurance applies or appears to apply` `workers compensation, catastrophic injuries or Defence Force law applies or appears to apply` `I am a veteran who elects the DVA arrangement` `I am a nursing home type patient` `my election` `I am an asylum seeker` `I am an involuntary patient under the Mental Health Act 2014` `the service is part of my Mental Health Act treatment` `a declaration under s 6(2) of the Health Insurance Act 1973 applies to me`) `how I attend`)
    WHEN `I am a family member or carer of a patient, or stay in hospital with one` THEN `does not arise for you`
    WHEN `I must pay compensation to someone a public hospital treated for their injury` THEN `does not arise for you`
    WHEN `I hold information about someone that I obtained through the WA health system` THEN `does not arise for you`

@export Am I free of all hospital fees and charges as a Medicare-ineligible asylum seeker or involuntary patient (Health Services (Fees and Charges) Order 2016 (WA) cl 6A)?
GIVEN `my role` IS A Role @desc Which of these describes you
      `how I attend` IS A `How I attend` @desc Whether you are an in-patient (admitted to a hospital bed), a day patient (at a day hospital), an out-patient, or a same day patient (admitted and discharged on the same day)
      `I am an eligible person under the Health Insurance Act 1973` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are an "eligible person" for Medicare under the Health Insurance Act 1973 s 3 (the Health Insurance Act encoding works this out)
      `I have, or appear to have, a right to compensation for this condition` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have received, or established a right to receive, compensation (such as workers' compensation, motor vehicle third party insurance or damages) for the injury, illness or disease you are treated for, or appear to have that right when you attend
      `the chief executive has decided I am not a compensable patient` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the hospital's chief executive has decided under Order cl 3A(5) that you are not a compensable patient
      `motor vehicle third party insurance applies or appears to apply` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Motor Vehicle (Third Party Insurance) Act 1943 applies, or appears to apply, to the payment for your treatment
      `workers compensation, catastrophic injuries or Defence Force law applies or appears to apply` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Workers Compensation and Injury Management Act 2023, the Motor Vehicle and Workplace Accidents (Catastrophic Injuries) Act 2016 or the law relating to the Defence Force applies, or appears to apply, to the payment for your treatment
      `I am a veteran who elects the DVA arrangement` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are a veteran who elects to be treated under the arrangement with the Department of Veterans' Affairs
      `I am a nursing home type patient` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are a nursing home type patient (a Health Insurance Act 1973 term for a long-stay in-patient who no longer needs acute care)
      `my election` IS A `My election` @desc Whether, on admission, you elected to be treated as a public or a private patient (an eligible in-patient or same day patient must elect)
      `I am an asylum seeker` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have applied for a permanent or temporary protection visa or a safe haven enterprise visa, and the application has not yet been decided
      `I am an involuntary patient under the Mental Health Act 2014` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are under an inpatient or community treatment order, another detaining order, or an unexpired referral under the Mental Health Act 2014
      `the service is part of my Mental Health Act treatment` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether this service is part of your treatment under the Mental Health Act 2014
      `a declaration under s 6(2) of the Health Insurance Act 1973 applies to me` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a declaration under the Health Insurance Act 1973 s 6(2) applies to you, so that you are treated as not an eligible person
GIVETH A `Yes or no`
`Am I free of all hospital fees and charges under cl 6A` MEANS
    CONSIDER `my role`
    WHEN `I am a patient of a WA public health service` THEN `yes if` (`Am I free of all fees under cl 6A` (`me from my answers` `I am an eligible person under the Health Insurance Act 1973` `I have, or appear to have, a right to compensation for this condition` `the chief executive has decided I am not a compensable patient` `motor vehicle third party insurance applies or appears to apply` `workers compensation, catastrophic injuries or Defence Force law applies or appears to apply` `I am a veteran who elects the DVA arrangement` `I am a nursing home type patient` `my election` `I am an asylum seeker` `I am an involuntary patient under the Mental Health Act 2014` `the service is part of my Mental Health Act treatment` `a declaration under s 6(2) of the Health Insurance Act 1973 applies to me`) `how I attend`)
    WHEN `I am a family member or carer of a patient, or stay in hospital with one` THEN `does not arise for you`
    WHEN `I must pay compensation to someone a public hospital treated for their injury` THEN `does not arise for you`
    WHEN `I hold information about someone that I obtained through the WA health system` THEN `does not arise for you`

@export What class of patient am I for hospital fees and charges (Health Services (Fees and Charges) Order 2016 (WA) cll 6A, 11-15)?
GIVEN `my role` IS A Role @desc Which of these describes you
      `how I attend` IS A `How I attend` @desc Whether you are an in-patient (admitted to a hospital bed), a day patient (at a day hospital), an out-patient, or a same day patient (admitted and discharged on the same day)
      `I am an eligible person under the Health Insurance Act 1973` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are an "eligible person" for Medicare under the Health Insurance Act 1973 s 3 (the Health Insurance Act encoding works this out)
      `I have, or appear to have, a right to compensation for this condition` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have received, or established a right to receive, compensation (such as workers' compensation, motor vehicle third party insurance or damages) for the injury, illness or disease you are treated for, or appear to have that right when you attend
      `the chief executive has decided I am not a compensable patient` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the hospital's chief executive has decided under Order cl 3A(5) that you are not a compensable patient
      `motor vehicle third party insurance applies or appears to apply` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Motor Vehicle (Third Party Insurance) Act 1943 applies, or appears to apply, to the payment for your treatment
      `workers compensation, catastrophic injuries or Defence Force law applies or appears to apply` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Workers Compensation and Injury Management Act 2023, the Motor Vehicle and Workplace Accidents (Catastrophic Injuries) Act 2016 or the law relating to the Defence Force applies, or appears to apply, to the payment for your treatment
      `I am a veteran who elects the DVA arrangement` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are a veteran who elects to be treated under the arrangement with the Department of Veterans' Affairs
      `I am a nursing home type patient` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are a nursing home type patient (a Health Insurance Act 1973 term for a long-stay in-patient who no longer needs acute care)
      `my election` IS A `My election` @desc Whether, on admission, you elected to be treated as a public or a private patient (an eligible in-patient or same day patient must elect)
      `I am an asylum seeker` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have applied for a permanent or temporary protection visa or a safe haven enterprise visa, and the application has not yet been decided
      `I am an involuntary patient under the Mental Health Act 2014` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are under an inpatient or community treatment order, another detaining order, or an unexpired referral under the Mental Health Act 2014
      `the service is part of my Mental Health Act treatment` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether this service is part of your treatment under the Mental Health Act 2014
      `a declaration under s 6(2) of the Health Insurance Act 1973 applies to me` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a declaration under the Health Insurance Act 1973 s 6(2) applies to you, so that you are treated as not an eligible person
GIVETH A `Class answer`
`What class of patient am I for hospital charges` MEANS
    CONSIDER `my role`
    WHEN `I am a patient of a WA public health service` THEN `my class is` (`What class of patient am I` (`me from my answers` `I am an eligible person under the Health Insurance Act 1973` `I have, or appear to have, a right to compensation for this condition` `the chief executive has decided I am not a compensable patient` `motor vehicle third party insurance applies or appears to apply` `workers compensation, catastrophic injuries or Defence Force law applies or appears to apply` `I am a veteran who elects the DVA arrangement` `I am a nursing home type patient` `my election` `I am an asylum seeker` `I am an involuntary patient under the Mental Health Act 2014` `the service is part of my Mental Health Act treatment` `a declaration under s 6(2) of the Health Insurance Act 1973 applies to me`) `how I attend`)
    WHEN `I am a family member or carer of a patient, or stay in hospital with one` THEN `does not arise for you`
    WHEN `I must pay compensation to someone a public hospital treated for their injury` THEN `does not arise for you`
    WHEN `I hold information about someone that I obtained through the WA health system` THEN `does not arise for you`

§§§ `Order cll 5, 6, Schedule 1 Divisions 1 and 2 -- what the hospital charges me`

@export I am admitted: what does the public hospital charge me per day for my stay (Health Services (Fees and Charges) Order 2016 (WA) cll 5, 6, 6A; Schedule 1 Divisions 1 and 2)?
GIVEN `my role` IS A Role @desc Which of these describes you
      `how I attend` IS A `How I attend` @desc Whether you are an in-patient (admitted to a hospital bed), a day patient (at a day hospital), an out-patient, or a same day patient (admitted and discharged on the same day)
      `I asked for a single bed ward` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are in a single-bed ward at your own request
      `bed` IS A `My bed` @desc The kind of bed and care (matters only for a compensable in-patient)
      `I am an eligible person under the Health Insurance Act 1973` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are an "eligible person" for Medicare under the Health Insurance Act 1973 s 3 (the Health Insurance Act encoding works this out)
      `I have, or appear to have, a right to compensation for this condition` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have received, or established a right to receive, compensation (such as workers' compensation, motor vehicle third party insurance or damages) for the injury, illness or disease you are treated for, or appear to have that right when you attend
      `the chief executive has decided I am not a compensable patient` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the hospital's chief executive has decided under Order cl 3A(5) that you are not a compensable patient
      `motor vehicle third party insurance applies or appears to apply` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Motor Vehicle (Third Party Insurance) Act 1943 applies, or appears to apply, to the payment for your treatment
      `workers compensation, catastrophic injuries or Defence Force law applies or appears to apply` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Workers Compensation and Injury Management Act 2023, the Motor Vehicle and Workplace Accidents (Catastrophic Injuries) Act 2016 or the law relating to the Defence Force applies, or appears to apply, to the payment for your treatment
      `I am a veteran who elects the DVA arrangement` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are a veteran who elects to be treated under the arrangement with the Department of Veterans' Affairs
      `I am a nursing home type patient` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are a nursing home type patient (a Health Insurance Act 1973 term for a long-stay in-patient who no longer needs acute care)
      `my election` IS A `My election` @desc Whether, on admission, you elected to be treated as a public or a private patient (an eligible in-patient or same day patient must elect)
      `I am an asylum seeker` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have applied for a permanent or temporary protection visa or a safe haven enterprise visa, and the application has not yet been decided
      `I am an involuntary patient under the Mental Health Act 2014` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are under an inpatient or community treatment order, another detaining order, or an unexpired referral under the Mental Health Act 2014
      `the service is part of my Mental Health Act treatment` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether this service is part of your treatment under the Mental Health Act 2014
      `a declaration under s 6(2) of the Health Insurance Act 1973 applies to me` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a declaration under the Health Insurance Act 1973 s 6(2) applies to you, so that you are treated as not an eligible person
GIVETH A `Charge answer`
`What does the public hospital charge me per day for my stay` MEANS
    CONSIDER `my role`
    WHEN `I am a patient of a WA public health service` THEN
        IF `I attend as an out-patient` `how I attend`
        THEN `does not arise for you`
        ELSE `the hospital's charge` (`What does the hospital charge me per day` (`me from my answers` `I am an eligible person under the Health Insurance Act 1973` `I have, or appear to have, a right to compensation for this condition` `the chief executive has decided I am not a compensable patient` `motor vehicle third party insurance applies or appears to apply` `workers compensation, catastrophic injuries or Defence Force law applies or appears to apply` `I am a veteran who elects the DVA arrangement` `I am a nursing home type patient` `my election` `I am an asylum seeker` `I am an involuntary patient under the Mental Health Act 2014` `the service is part of my Mental Health Act treatment` `a declaration under s 6(2) of the Health Insurance Act 1973 applies to me`) `how I attend` `I asked for a single bed ward` `bed`)
    WHEN `I am a family member or carer of a patient, or stay in hospital with one` THEN `does not arise for you`
    WHEN `I must pay compensation to someone a public hospital treated for their injury` THEN `does not arise for you`
    WHEN `I hold information about someone that I obtained through the WA health system` THEN `does not arise for you`

@export I am admitted: what will my whole stay cost me under the Order, in dollars, at most (Health Services (Fees and Charges) Order 2016 (WA) Schedule 1 Divisions 1 and 2)?
GIVEN `my role` IS A Role @desc Which of these describes you
      `how I attend` IS A `How I attend` @desc Whether you are an in-patient (admitted to a hospital bed), a day patient (at a day hospital), an out-patient, or a same day patient (admitted and discharged on the same day)
      `I asked for a single bed ward` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are in a single-bed ward at your own request
      `bed` IS A `My bed` @desc The kind of bed and care (matters only for a compensable in-patient)
      `days` IS A NUMBER @desc How many days you are charged for
      `I am an eligible person under the Health Insurance Act 1973` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are an "eligible person" for Medicare under the Health Insurance Act 1973 s 3 (the Health Insurance Act encoding works this out)
      `I have, or appear to have, a right to compensation for this condition` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have received, or established a right to receive, compensation (such as workers' compensation, motor vehicle third party insurance or damages) for the injury, illness or disease you are treated for, or appear to have that right when you attend
      `the chief executive has decided I am not a compensable patient` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the hospital's chief executive has decided under Order cl 3A(5) that you are not a compensable patient
      `motor vehicle third party insurance applies or appears to apply` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Motor Vehicle (Third Party Insurance) Act 1943 applies, or appears to apply, to the payment for your treatment
      `workers compensation, catastrophic injuries or Defence Force law applies or appears to apply` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Workers Compensation and Injury Management Act 2023, the Motor Vehicle and Workplace Accidents (Catastrophic Injuries) Act 2016 or the law relating to the Defence Force applies, or appears to apply, to the payment for your treatment
      `I am a veteran who elects the DVA arrangement` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are a veteran who elects to be treated under the arrangement with the Department of Veterans' Affairs
      `I am a nursing home type patient` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are a nursing home type patient (a Health Insurance Act 1973 term for a long-stay in-patient who no longer needs acute care)
      `my election` IS A `My election` @desc Whether, on admission, you elected to be treated as a public or a private patient (an eligible in-patient or same day patient must elect)
      `I am an asylum seeker` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have applied for a permanent or temporary protection visa or a safe haven enterprise visa, and the application has not yet been decided
      `I am an involuntary patient under the Mental Health Act 2014` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are under an inpatient or community treatment order, another detaining order, or an unexpired referral under the Mental Health Act 2014
      `the service is part of my Mental Health Act treatment` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether this service is part of your treatment under the Mental Health Act 2014
      `a declaration under s 6(2) of the Health Insurance Act 1973 applies to me` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a declaration under the Health Insurance Act 1973 s 6(2) applies to you, so that you are treated as not an eligible person
GIVETH AN `Amount`
`What will my whole stay cost me under the Order` MEANS
    CONSIDER `my role`
    WHEN `I am a patient of a WA public health service` THEN
        IF `I attend as an out-patient` `how I attend`
        THEN `does not arise for you`
        ELSE amount (`What will my stay cost under the Order` (`me from my answers` `I am an eligible person under the Health Insurance Act 1973` `I have, or appear to have, a right to compensation for this condition` `the chief executive has decided I am not a compensable patient` `motor vehicle third party insurance applies or appears to apply` `workers compensation, catastrophic injuries or Defence Force law applies or appears to apply` `I am a veteran who elects the DVA arrangement` `I am a nursing home type patient` `my election` `I am an asylum seeker` `I am an involuntary patient under the Mental Health Act 2014` `the service is part of my Mental Health Act treatment` `a declaration under s 6(2) of the Health Insurance Act 1973 applies to me`) `how I attend` `I asked for a single bed ward` `bed` `days`)
    WHEN `I am a family member or carer of a patient, or stay in hospital with one` THEN `does not arise for you`
    WHEN `I must pay compensation to someone a public hospital treated for their injury` THEN `does not arise for you`
    WHEN `I hold information about someone that I obtained through the WA health system` THEN `does not arise for you`

@export I am an out-patient: what does the public hospital charge me for each out-patient service (Health Services (Fees and Charges) Order 2016 (WA) Schedule 1 Division 1 item 4, Division 2 items 7, 9)?
GIVEN `my role` IS A Role @desc Which of these describes you
      `how I attend` IS A `How I attend` @desc Whether you are an in-patient (admitted to a hospital bed), a day patient (at a day hospital), an out-patient, or a same day patient (admitted and discharged on the same day)
      `I am an eligible person under the Health Insurance Act 1973` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are an "eligible person" for Medicare under the Health Insurance Act 1973 s 3 (the Health Insurance Act encoding works this out)
      `I have, or appear to have, a right to compensation for this condition` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have received, or established a right to receive, compensation (such as workers' compensation, motor vehicle third party insurance or damages) for the injury, illness or disease you are treated for, or appear to have that right when you attend
      `the chief executive has decided I am not a compensable patient` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the hospital's chief executive has decided under Order cl 3A(5) that you are not a compensable patient
      `motor vehicle third party insurance applies or appears to apply` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Motor Vehicle (Third Party Insurance) Act 1943 applies, or appears to apply, to the payment for your treatment
      `workers compensation, catastrophic injuries or Defence Force law applies or appears to apply` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Workers Compensation and Injury Management Act 2023, the Motor Vehicle and Workplace Accidents (Catastrophic Injuries) Act 2016 or the law relating to the Defence Force applies, or appears to apply, to the payment for your treatment
      `I am a veteran who elects the DVA arrangement` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are a veteran who elects to be treated under the arrangement with the Department of Veterans' Affairs
      `I am a nursing home type patient` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are a nursing home type patient (a Health Insurance Act 1973 term for a long-stay in-patient who no longer needs acute care)
      `my election` IS A `My election` @desc Whether, on admission, you elected to be treated as a public or a private patient (an eligible in-patient or same day patient must elect)
      `I am an asylum seeker` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have applied for a permanent or temporary protection visa or a safe haven enterprise visa, and the application has not yet been decided
      `I am an involuntary patient under the Mental Health Act 2014` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are under an inpatient or community treatment order, another detaining order, or an unexpired referral under the Mental Health Act 2014
      `the service is part of my Mental Health Act treatment` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether this service is part of your treatment under the Mental Health Act 2014
      `a declaration under s 6(2) of the Health Insurance Act 1973 applies to me` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a declaration under the Health Insurance Act 1973 s 6(2) applies to you, so that you are treated as not an eligible person
GIVETH A `Charge answer`
`What does the public hospital charge me for each out-patient service` MEANS
    CONSIDER `my role`
    WHEN `I am a patient of a WA public health service` THEN
        IF `I attend as an out-patient` `how I attend`
        THEN `the hospital's charge` (`What does the hospital charge me for an out-patient service` (`me from my answers` `I am an eligible person under the Health Insurance Act 1973` `I have, or appear to have, a right to compensation for this condition` `the chief executive has decided I am not a compensable patient` `motor vehicle third party insurance applies or appears to apply` `workers compensation, catastrophic injuries or Defence Force law applies or appears to apply` `I am a veteran who elects the DVA arrangement` `I am a nursing home type patient` `my election` `I am an asylum seeker` `I am an involuntary patient under the Mental Health Act 2014` `the service is part of my Mental Health Act treatment` `a declaration under s 6(2) of the Health Insurance Act 1973 applies to me`))
        ELSE `does not arise for you`
    WHEN `I am a family member or carer of a patient, or stay in hospital with one` THEN `does not arise for you`
    WHEN `I must pay compensation to someone a public hospital treated for their injury` THEN `does not arise for you`
    WHEN `I hold information about someone that I obtained through the WA health system` THEN `does not arise for you`

@export I am an out-patient: what does the public hospital charge me for each out-patient medicine item (Health Services (Fees and Charges) Order 2016 (WA) Schedule 1 Division 1 item 5, Division 2 item 8, cl 10A)?
